<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of South Carolina</h1><p class="st-meta">1712 CE – 1776 CE · capital Charles Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The richest colony in British North America, whose rice and indigo plantations around Charles Town depended on an enslaved African majority.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The southern settlements of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Province of Carolina</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charleston-south-carolina">Charles Town</a>, received a separate governor in 1712. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamasee-war">Yamasee War</a> of 1715 to 1717 nearly destroyed the colony, settlers overthrew proprietary rule in 1719 and asked for royal government, which the Crown formalised in 1729.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> grown in tidal swamps, and from the 1740s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eliza-lucas-pinckney">Eliza Lucas Pinckney</a>, made the planter elite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina-lowcountry">Lowcountry</a> the wealthiest in British America. Enslaved Africans formed the majority of the population, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gullah">Gullah</a> language and culture took shape on the coastal plantations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stono-rebellion">Stono Rebellion</a> of 1739 led to harsh new slave laws. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charleston-south-carolina">Charles Town</a> was a lively port with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>, libraries and the deerskin trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last royal governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-william-campbell">Lord William Campbell</a>, fled in 1775. In March 1776 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> adopted its own constitution, and in June its defenders repulsed a British fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sullivans-island">Sullivan's Island</a>. It became a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-south-carolina.jpg" alt="Province of South Carolina" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AnonMoos, based on image by Zscout370, AnonMoos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_ensign_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800,_square_canton).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1712 CE – 1776 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Charles Town</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert Johnson, James Glen, William Campbell</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Gullah</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>South Carolina (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1744&amp;focus=province-of-south-carolina" aria-label="Province of South Carolina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2616 945 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of South Carolina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2936 1201L2935 1168M2936 1201L4997 651M2936 1201L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2935 1168h0M4997 651h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2936 1201h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2936 1201h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1744&amp;focus=province-of-south-carolina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
